xon fully supported that ambition and  in 1972 gave the shaw the keys to the  store  full access to buy u.s weapons with his  oil money  the result was a fantastic build up  including an influx of 25 000  american technicians into parts of iran  that had hardly seen westerners  it was not a good mix  and many objective experts believe and  iranians particularly i've talked to  about this  that it aroused feelings  that made the eventual iranian  revolution  against the shah in 1978 and 1979  much more extreme and anti-american than  it would otherwise have been  even after 20 years  the national  shame  of iran's seizure of american hostages  in 1979  and the hostage and the hardships they  endured held captive for a year and a  half  should remind us of unwise decision  decisions in the nixon era  if iran was a disaster policy area  however  the nixon administration's handling of  the arab israeli problem was a brilliant  success  in 1970 nixon and kissinger worked  closely with mrs mayer  and robin  getchakrabin to thwart a determined plo  effort to take over jordan with syria  joining in already too  a fine example of effective crisis  management  then as we've already noted  in 1973 anwar sadat in egypt attacked  israel  catching israeli forces unprepared and  also achieving what might be called  strategic surprise  by enlisting oil-rich saudi arabia to  unleash what was called the oil weapon  namely the oil embargo which hit america  and its allies hard  and threatened the western alliance  with nixon completely distracted by  watergate  it fell to henry kissinger to conduct  what came to be called shuttle diplomacy  that first ended the war with bearable  withdrawal agreements  and then country by country  brought the moderate arab countries  around  not only to let up on the oil huago but  to draw closer to the u.s  and away from the radical arab states  and movements  these basic changes for which kissinger  deserves enormous credit  were to be the foundation for all later  efforts to move toward peace between the  arab countries and israel  elusive as that peace remains  to this day  precisely to this day one might almost  say with a y plantation  overall  how should one judge the nixon  administration's foreign policies  the conclusion has to be mixed certainly  by no means the string of successes  nixon and kissinger sought to portray  in their massive memoirs  i have three main conclusions  first that while both nixon and  kissinger knew a great deal about the  world  their judgment was erratic often far too  subjective and emotional  or driven by general concepts that did  not fit with underlying reality  second while nixon could be a master  strategist and planner he lacked the  qualities  of a statesman or builder  in contrast for example to harry truman  a much less sophisticated man though  well advised by george marshall and dean  atchison he built no lasting structures  and did not enlist the congressional and  public support necessary to sustain such  structures  third and most telling of all  his on nixon's unshakable bent to  secrecy and deception  undermined his accomplishments and in  the end brought him down  not just in the political sense  watergate but in the policy sense  in short  while it was a memorable and important  period in american foreign policy  it was not  one to emulate  or in the end admire  finally if i have a minute or two more  the nixon era is a case study directly  relevant to the quest to a question we  face today  the effect of a presidential scandal on  foreign policy  the initial actions of watergate crimes  by any legal standard  were mostly burglaries to get  politically useful material for use  against democrats or critics of policy  these started in 1971 nixon's third year  in office and blossomed in 1972 and 1973  into a full-scale program  finally revealed in the spring of 1973  by the confession  of a man named james mccord  who had participated in the june 1972  burglary of democratic party national  headquarters in the watergate building  complex in washington  this triggered  senate committee hearings focused on  cover-up evidence  and then step by step  to nixon firing the first special  prosecutor archibald cox  and his attorney general elliot  richardson who refused to obey the order  to fire cox  in this so-called saturday night  massacre of october 1973  this was followed relentlessly  by the appointment of a new special  prosecutor  leon jaworski  a veteran  non-political trial lawyer and past  president of the american bar  association  the house judiciary committee committee  after receiving from a grand jury  the evidence cox and jaworski had  obtained  secrecy so total that the committee was  not even informed that the grand jury  had named nixon himself as an unindicted  co-conspirator  they went ahead the committee  to consider careful and again secret  summaries of the evidence and in july  1974  voted three articles of impeachment  rejecting two others when crossover  democratic committee members voted with  the republican minority  one of these  was the valid charge that nixon had  falsely dedicated the papers  on a large transfer of his official  papers to the government which he  claimed as a deductible gift on his  income tax  the committee concluded that since this  was not an official act it should not be  part of impeachment articles  at that climactic point  nixon was forced by a supreme court  decision  to turn over what came to be called the  smoking gun tape that showed him  ordering the cia director six days after  the burglary  to claim falsely the fbi investigations  of the of that main burglary would  interfere with cia secret operations  with the house clearly ready to propose  impeachment in the senate to vote it  nixon had no choice but to resign  end to end from mccord's confession to  the final committee votes  watergate watergate lasted 16 plunks  plus months  from every angle except possibly the  degree of media attention  it was a far bigger scandal than the  present clinton star won  in its last months nixon lost his powers  of concentration especially on the  middle east war  but he was fortunate in having kissinger  to take over  so far as far as i can tell anyway  we have seen no comparable effects in  foreign policy  but the impact of watergate  as in in that from that period  was enough to provide a strong historic  argument for dealing with the present  scandal as quickly as possible  thank you very much  thank you for being our legacy to  history  i'd like to open the florida questions  and i would ask you to please identify  yourself and keep your question brief  and to the point  arlen was an international institute for  entrepreneurship development in wilkes  university  you described nixon as  highly prone to secrecy and deception  and you also describe nixon and  kissinger as what sounds like an  imperial  presidency with the richelieu  agenda  how would you interpret  nixon today and in terms of his  his need to dominate or to  prevent information from coming out or  to cause  his policies to be  followed without any question or not to  consult others to any degree  you're inviting me to have an  analysis of his psychology in effect  and there are people who've done that i  didn't do it in the book i deliberately  stayed away from that  there are those who go back to his  boyhood in california where he was one  of the have-nots in his high school  class this kind of thing  certainly he had a  chip on his shoulder toward people that  he thought had had more advantages than  he had  and that was a factor too  um  then  i think the  the taste for secrecy  which was so basic  was already present uh i mean he  he did it i i paused to say that i think  he did an honorable and correct job in  the alger his investigation  in contrast to almost all that mccarthy  was doing in the same period  but he  and nixon developed this this bent for  secrecy  particularly in the eisenhower  administration's frequent  covert operations  the last one of which eisenhower mounted  was what became the bay of pigs under  kennedy  and nixon had supported that  he supported  that kind of action  and another thing he supported he came  to support in the eisenhower period  was the use of the threat of heavy  bombing  as a way of resolving crises  he was convinced  and i don't think the evidence really  supports this as strongly as he believed  it anyway  that in 19  see how i get this date straight in 19  when eisenhower  got the ceasefire in korea  that the decisive factor  was a threat of using nuclear weapons  conveyed i think through the indian  ambassador  nixon believed that that kind of  dramatic threat  was a very decisive way of acting  and did it himself  vainly in 1969 briefly in an ultimatum  he gave the north vietnamese  those all those  elements of his  character and i've used examples from  foreign policy  were deeply rooted and  they continued to be i suppose i haven't  followed them  in in areas of domestic policy or  but you can you can see it  uh his constant  feeling for  the next political maneuver he could  make  one of the most revealing sources in the  work i did was  the diaries of h.r haldeman  nixon's chief of staff  and  the these reveal the degree to which  nixon would lock himself up or not lock  himself but but isolate himself in the  executive office building  and write out all kinds of ideas of  people he could talk to influence  manipulate  and then give the list to haldeman to  pursue  that was  that was his his nature and his practice  in the political arena and it to the  maximum degree he could  in the foreign policy one  yes  uh richard valcourt international  journal of intelligence um i was struck  by your inclusion of leonard brezhnev as  one of the outstanding leaders of that  period it's become fashionable to have a  void between nikita khrushchev and the  kyle gorbachev so could you elaborate a  little bit on your assessment of  brushnap for that long period that he  ruled russia  no i wouldn't be able to mount a  full-scale  appraisal of his entire tenure which  varied enormously i think he was just an  apparatchik or someone judged for a very  long period  but i think one can see him emerging in  the nixon period  as a man who thought independently or at  least to a degree independently that's  relative word of course in the soviet  decision-making structure  but he seemed to be truly in charge  on many occasions  which was not the case at the beginning  and i think had not been the case in  earlier periods  you can see that  things that seem to be authentically  brezhnev  included for example and this was a  rather dramatic episode  in 1973 in the second summit  the summit held here return engagement  brezhnev  summoned nixon in the middle of the  night out at san clemente  to urge him in the strongest terms  to get the arab-israel situation  resolved so that there wouldn't be a war  i think that was brezhnev  thinking he could use the situation at a  particular moment his tie with nixon  which by then had become quite close  to get the u.s to intervene  and  nip the 1973 war preparation is already  visible in the bud  now i may be wrong we don't have the  soviet papers that really show who was  powerful and who was not  but my reading is that nixon  not nixon brezhnev of course i mean  uh was increasingly powerful in the  soviet structure throughout this period  i think you've got jim billington come  coming a little  later in the month and maybe i'd better  say ask him  my old friend  oh yes i'm sorry mr bundy  chester berger  would you care to say anything about  the  abdominal theory as a factor in  development of policy in southeast asia  and subsequently  what you think the last period is shown  about it  right well that's right on point of  course and i have a have a couple of  pages  devoted to that precise question  uh in the book  of course the domino theory was not  invoked it really dated back to  the kennedy period  into 1964 65  uh  in its in it that was its simplified  version i think that the phrase was ice  with eisenhower's  uh  but the way it was finally and it's more  sophisticated form it was that the  uh  overall communist threat in southeast  asia  china on the one hand in north vietname  vietnam on the other even though they  were not regarded  as a team or that north china was in  charge  that they would put pressure on what  were in fact very certainly very  vulnerable and unstable non-communist  nations  and that if  the vietnam war turned out in the favor  of the communist side this would be  enormously  increased  certainly  it had a certain plausibility to it  i never liked it i never never liked the  phrasing certainly i never you don't  think i ever used the word domino theory  in my years in office  but  the  feeling in southeast asia at the time  was indeed palpable  and my personal index of it was quite  largely  the then i thought very attractive and  certainly interesting character lee kuan  yew in singapore  who was neutral  and remained neutral but who gave me a  tremendous lecture and i'll never forget  it  that we had to stand or the wind would  blow from the east was the phrase he  used  that was the feeling of the nations in  the area  and that was a great part of the  evidence that led us to attach very  significant weight to this factor  now  jumping ahead  i have described in the book how when  after  congress had  ended u.s participation  and in a succession of situations the  communists again went on the offensive  and of course captured saigon and took  over the whole country in 1975  that was  many people took that to me  and  and then the  critical bit after that  is that this the dominoes didn't fall  that the southeast asian nations did not  turn communist or give any particular  sign of doing so in 1975.  so many people have drawn the  conclusion that the domino theory in its  simplified or sophisticated forms  was just simply not valid  i've expressed my view that at  particular times in 6465 was one of them  it did seem to me to have considerable  weight  and i believe  and i tried this at a conference of  historians about three years ago and i  was surprised to find that they listened  carefully  that  the  steps that we took our presence in  indochina  they are carrying on with the war  did  help to improve confidence  in the intervening years  so that what had been particularly weak  and vulnerable nations in southeast asia  were by 1975 considerably stronger  now that doesn't say for a minute  that  the war was worth its costs of all sorts  but that there was this one aspect of  gain  now  to get to that point you have to analyze  the crucial change that took place in  southeast asia in 6566  which was the  dramatic  ouster of sicarno and his replacement by  a man who then appeared to be quote a  good guy  suharto  and  that changeover which we had no reason  to expect did not expect and responded  to by a policy of total silence  was an enormous change in the whole  makeup of southeast asia and if you let  me run on for a moment longer  i vividly recall  speaking across the street at the  council in spring of 1966  on the situation as it then appeared  and having george cannon in the front  row  and george  held up his hand and i naturally called  on him  he said don't you think  that now with the changed situation in  indonesia  the factors that led to the american  intervention are now so much weakened  that we could afford to turn to a much  less participatory and involved policy  i'm afraid i made sputtering noises  about china still being a threat  i think george was emphatically right  it was what general gavin was saying at  the time from the military standpoint  hunker down and protect what you've got  on the coast but don't try to  really win the war militarily  it was also what another man whom